# Real 404s. This used to be `/*  /index.html  200`, which returned HTTP 200
# with the homepage for every wrong URL — so any typo, dead backlink or
# crawler probe produced another copy of the front page at a new address.
# Google reads that as a soft 404 and, at worst, as duplicate content across
# unlimited URLs. Serving 404.html with a 404 status ends it.
/404       /404.html  404
/404/      /404.html  404

# The splat rules, kept together here after every static rule. Cloudflare Pages
# allows 2,000 static rules but only 100 dynamic ones, its docs say static
# redirects should come before dynamic ones, and it counts every rule after
# the first splat as dynamic. While /unsubscribe/* sat near the top, the rules
# under it passed 100 once the C4i and article redirects arrived, and wrangler
# dropped the last 10 without an error, /welcome and /CaseStudy among them
# (reproduced 9 Oct 2026). Moved down the same day. No static rule above
# matches a path under /unsubscribe/ or /SoftwareProduct/, so the move didn't
# change where any path goes.
#
# Unsubscribe links in outreach email point at c4studios.com.au rather than a
# supabase.co URL: recipients (and school mail filters) trust the known domain.
# The function opts them out, then redirects to /unsubscribed, which IS
# prerendered — hence the path here is /unsubscribe/*, not /unsubscribed/*.
#
# The token MUST stay in the destination path. Cloudflare Pages does not
# substitute :splat inside a destination query string: `?u=:splat` forwarded the
# literal "%3Asplat" and every emailed link died on badlink. Verified 12 Aug.
/unsubscribe/*  https://hauwhplevypinplfbbgh.supabase.co/functions/v1/unsubscribe/:splat  302

